(- Structure: If + Simple Past \(Subjunctive 'were' for all persons\), ... would /) Tj
T*
(could / might + Base Verb.) Tj
T*
(- Example: "If the laboratory were equipped with cryogenic electron microscopy, we) Tj
T*
(could resolve the macromolecular structure.") Tj
T*
(* Third Conditional \(Counterfactual Past Regret / Impossible Condition\):) Tj
T*
(- Structure: If + Past Perfect \(had + V3\), ... would have / could have + Past) Tj
T*
(Participle \(V3\).) Tj
T*
(- Example: "If the technician had calibrated the pH electrode yesterday, the) Tj
T*
(entire batch would not have spoiled.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([EXERCISE 1: Tense Consistency in Scientific Research Manuscripts]) Tj
T*
(Problem: Re-write the following experimental report excerpt, correcting inappropriate) Tj
T*
(shifts between past, present, and perfect tenses:) Tj
T*
(Inconsistent Draft:) Tj
T*
("The researchers synthesize [1] a series of novel bio-polymers. In the current paper, we) Tj
T*
(tested [2] their tensile strength. The results show [3] that polymer A had [4] an) Tj
T*
(ultimate tensile strength of 55 MPa. We conclude [5] that bio-polymers will be [6]) Tj
T*
(viable replacements for petrochemical plastics.") Tj
T*
(Corrected Academic Version:) Tj
T*
("The researchers synthesized [1, Simple Past for completed past synthesis] a series of) Tj
T*
(novel bio-polymers. In the current paper, we report [2, Simple Present for current) Tj
T*
(manuscript contents] their tensile strength. The results show [3, Simple Present for) Tj
T*
(timeless data presentation] that polymer A has [4, Simple Present for established) Tj
T*
(material property] an ultimate tensile strength of 55 MPa. We conclude [5, Simple) Tj
T*
(Present for author deduction] that these bio-polymers are [6, Simple Present for) Tj
T*
(generalized scientific conclusion] viable replacements for petrochemical plastics.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([EXERCISE 2: Conditional Clause Transformation Drill]) Tj
T*
(Problem: Transform the provided prompt across all four conditional forms, maintaining) Tj
T*
(semantic coherence:) Tj
T*
(Base Context: Autoclaving at 121 deg C destroys bacterial endospores.) Tj
T*
(- Zero Conditional: "If an autoclave maintains 121 deg C for 20 minutes, it) Tj
T*
(destroys all bacterial endospores.") Tj
T*
(- First Conditional: "If the technician runs the autoclave at 121 deg C for 20) Tj
T*
(minutes, the steam will destroy all bacterial endospores in the media.") Tj
T*
(- Second Conditional: "If this older autoclave maintained a steady 121 deg C, it) Tj
T*
(would destroy the persistent bacterial endospores.") Tj
T*
(- Third Conditional: "If the autoclave had maintained 121 deg C for the full 20) Tj
T*
(minutes, the steam would have destroyed the contaminating endospores.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([EXERCISE 3: Disambiguating 'Since' vs. 'For' in Longitudinal Studies]) Tj
T*
(Problem: Insert 'since' or 'for' accurately into the following clinical research) Tj
T*
(sentences:) Tj
T*
(Sentence 1: "The stem cell line has been continuously subcultured in our laboratory) Tj
T*
([since / for] over six years.") Tj
T*
(- Solution: FOR. \('over six years' denotes an accumulated duration of time\).) Tj
T*
ET

