([EXERCISE 4: Subjunctive Mood in Hypothetical Conditionals]) Tj
T*
(Problem: Correct the grammatical error in the following conditional statement,) Tj
T*
(explaining the rule of the Subjunctive Mood:) Tj
T*
(Faulty Sentence: "If the enzyme concentration was doubled, the initial velocity would) Tj
T*
(increase proportionally.") Tj
T*
(- Correction: "If the enzyme concentration WERE doubled, the initial velocity) Tj
T*
(would increase proportionally.") Tj
T*
(- Linguistic Rule: In hypothetical \(Second Conditional\) constructions expressing) Tj
T*
(unreal or contrary-to-fact scenarios, the past subjunctive form 'were' is) Tj
T*
(mandatory for all grammatical persons \(singular and plural: I were, he were, it) Tj
T*
(were\). The indicative 'was' is considered informal and ungrammatical in formal) Tj
T*
(scientific prose.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
