/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 3.3 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Tabulate the four types of conditional clauses in English, showing their) Tj
T*
(if-clause structures, main clause structures, and practical functions with scientific) Tj
T*
(examples.) Tj
T*
(Answer: \(1\) Zero Conditional: If-Clause: If + Simple Present; Main Clause: Simple) Tj
T*
(Present. Function: Universal scientific laws and invariant physical facts. Example: 'If) Tj
T*
(heat is applied to ice, it melts.' \(2\) First Conditional: If-Clause: If + Simple) Tj
T*
(Present; Main Clause: will / can + Base Verb \(V1\). Function: Real or highly probable) Tj
T*
(future events based on present conditions. Example: 'If the primer concentration is too) Tj
T*
(low, the PCR reaction will fail.' \(3\) Second Conditional: If-Clause: If + Simple Past) Tj
T*
(\(Subjunctive were\); Main Clause: would / could + Base Verb \(V1\). Function: Hypothetical,) Tj
T*
(unlikely, or contrary-to-fact present/future scenarios. Example: 'If the enzyme were) Tj
T*
(thermostable at 95 deg C, it would survive repeated denaturation cycles.' \(4\) Third) Tj
T*
(Conditional: If-Clause: If + Past Perfect \(had + V3\); Main Clause: would have + Past) Tj
T*
(Participle \(V3\). Function: Counterfactual past events that did not happen and cannot be) Tj
T*
(changed. Example: 'If the sample had been stored at -80 deg C, the RNA would not have) Tj
T*
(degraded.') Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the conventional distribution of tenses across different sections of a) Tj
T*
(standard IMRaD scientific research paper.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Standard scientific publications adhere to the IMRaD \(Introduction, Methods,) Tj
T*
(Results, and Discussion\) structural convention: \(1\) Introduction: Simple Present for) Tj
T*
